# Core Vibe Coding Workflow (Go + Gin)

## Purpose
This rule defines the fundamental workflow for phase-based development with test-driven implementation in Go/Gin projects. Always apply these principles regardless of the specific task.

## Workflow Phases

All feature development follows this structure:

1. **Architect Phase**: Design OpenAPI specification and session plan
2. **Phase 0 (Skeleton)**: Create all endpoints with mock responses
3. **Phase 1+ (Implementation)**: Implement each endpoint following TDD
4. **Phase Transition**: Generate summary and document learnings

## Golden Rules

### Always Set Up the Go Module First
Before any development work:
- Initialize the module: `go mod init module/path` (only once, at project creation)
- Keep `go.mod`/`go.sum` tidy: `go mod tidy` after every dependency change
- Use `go run ./cmd/server` or a built binary for all execution — never hand-edit `go.sum`
- Vendor only if the course explicitly requires reproducible offline builds (`go mod vendor`)

### Always Read Vibe Guides First
Before starting any phase, read the relevant guide from the vibe/ folder:
- vibe_phase_workflow.md - Phase-based development patterns
- vibe_gin_boilerplate.md - Project structure and boilerplate patterns
- vibe_database.md - GORM models and repository patterns
- vibe_development_lifecycle.md - Git workflow and deployment
- vibe_docker.md - Containerization patterns (already stack-complete, cross-reference it)

### Test-Driven Development (TDD)
- E2E tests (via `net/http/httptest`) MUST be written before implementation
- Tests MUST fail initially (proves they test something real)
- Implementation MUST make tests pass
- Tests MUST pass before phase transition: `go test ./... -v`

### Code Style Guidelines
Follow idiomatic Go conventions (Effective Go + Go Code Review Comments):
- `gofmt`/`goimports` clean on every file — no exceptions
- `go vet ./...` and `golangci-lint run` must be clean before phase transition
- Exported identifiers (types, funcs, consts) MUST have a doc comment starting with the identifier's name
- Errors are always the last return value; check `if err != nil` immediately, do not defer error handling
- Short, idiomatic names for local scope (`ctx`, `req`, `svc`, `repo`); descriptive names for package-level exports
- Wrap errors with context using `fmt.Errorf("doing x: %w", err)`; never swallow errors silently
- No naked panics in HTTP handlers — recover via middleware, return proper HTTP error responses

### Phase Transition Checklist
Before transitioning to next phase:
- [ ] All E2E tests passing (`go test ./... -v`)
- [ ] `go vet ./...` clean
- [ ] `golangci-lint run` clean
- [ ] `gofmt -l .` returns no files (fully formatted)
- [ ] Doc comments added to all new exported identifiers
- [ ] Phase summary generated
- [ ] Shared repositories/services documented

## Directory Structure Enforcement

When creating files, follow this structure:
```
cmd/
└── server/
    └── main.go          # Entrypoint: router, middleware, graceful shutdown
internal/
├── config/               # Configuration loading (viper/envconfig)
├── database/              # DB connection, pooling, migrations
├── middleware/             # Gin middleware (recovery, CORS, request-id, logging)
├── handler/                # Gin handlers (Experience layer, HTTP <-> DTO)
│   └── {feature}.go
├── model/                  # GORM models (DB layer) + API DTOs
│   └── {feature}.go
├── repository/              # Interfaces + GORM/sqlx implementations
│   └── {feature}_repository.go
└── service/                 # Business logic, orchestrates repositories
    └── {feature}_service.go
pkg/                        # Only truly reusable, externally-importable code
tests/                       # Or *_test.go colocated with source — pick one and be consistent
```

`internal/` is enforced by the Go compiler (not importable outside this module) — this is why business
logic lives there. `pkg/` is reserved for code you deliberately want other modules to import; most
course projects will have an empty or near-empty `pkg/`.

## Error Handling

When encountering unknown issues:
1. Document the problem clearly
2. List what has been tried
3. Collect relevant code snippets and `go vet`/compiler output
4. Generate research-export.md for external review
5. Wait for human input before major refactoring

## Secrets Management

NEVER commit secrets to the repository:
- Use `.env` files for local development (loaded via `viper` or `godotenv`)
- Reference `.env.example` for required variables
- Use environment variables in `docker-compose.yml`
- Validate all required secrets at startup — fail fast with a clear error if `DATABASE_URL` or similar is missing

## Response Format

When generating phase summaries or documentation:
- Use clear, professional language
- Include concrete Go code examples
- Reference specific files and line numbers
- List dependencies for next phases
- Note any gotchas or learnings (e.g. GORM quirks, Gin binding edge cases)
